{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "קמיך", :translit "kamekha", :gloss "Your foes"} {:he "תהרוס קמיך", :translit "takhros kamekha", :gloss "you destroy Your foes"} {:he "שאון קמיך עולה תמיד", :translit "sha'on kamekha oleh tamid", :gloss "the roar of Your foes rises continually"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["תהרוס קמיך" "שאון קמיך עולה תמיד"]}], :frame {:text "The phrase תהרוס קמיך appears in Exodus 15:7 and in Isaiah 17:12–14, where שאון קמיך עולה תמיד describes the nations who roar against God. Ba'al HaTurim links the two occurrences by way of gezah shavah, a method of verbal analogy where shared rare phrases invite interpretive connection.", :sources ["Exodus 15:7" "Isaiah 17:12"]}} {:quotes [{:he "תשלח", :translit "tishlakh", :gloss "You send"} {:he "ה' תשלח חרונך", :translit "Adonai tishlakh kharonekha", :gloss "YHWH, You send Your wrath"} {:he "מבצריהם תשלח", :translit "mivtzareihem tishlakh", :gloss "from their fortresses You send"} {:he "שלח תשלח את האם", :translit "shaleakh tishlakh et ha'em", :gloss "you shall surely send the mother"} {:he "תשלח רוחך יבראון", :translit "tishlakh ru'akha yibbare'un", :gloss "You send Your Spirit, they are created"} {:he "תשלח קציריה עד ים", :translit "tishlakh ketzireihah ad yam", :gloss "You send her harvest to the sea"} {:he "שלח תשלח את האם ואת הבנים תקח לך", :translit "shaleakh tishlakh et ha'em ve'et ha-banim tikakh lekha", :gloss "you shall surely send the mother and the young you shall take for yourself"} {:he "תשלח רוחך יבראון", :translit "tishlakh ru'akha yibbare'un", :gloss "You send Your Spirit, they are created"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "gezah shavah linking multiple occurrences of תשלח across verses"}], :frame {:text "Ba'al HaTurim threads several occurrences of the root שלח across Scripture. The reference to שלח תשלח את האם ואת הבנים תקח לך from Deuteronomy 22:6–7 is read as an allegory for God sending the father-generation to die in the wilderness while bringing the children into the land, as described in Numbers 14:29–31. The citation תשלח רוחך יבראון from Psalm 104:30 supports the claim that the wilderness generation still receives a portion in the World to Come. The comparison between the plagues of Egypt and the destruction at the sea draws on the precedent set in Exodus 14:31 and Deuteronomy 11:4, which link God's hand in Egypt to His hand at the sea.", :sources ["Deuteronomy 22:6" "Numbers 14:29" "Psalm 104:30" "Exodus 14:31" "Deuteronomy 11:4"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["תהרוס קמיך" "שאון קמיך עולה תמיד"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "תהרוס קמיך", :refs ["Exodus 15:7"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "שאון קמיך עולה תמיד", :refs ["Psalms 74:23"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :other, :note "gezah shavah linking multiple occurrences of תשלח across verses", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :exodus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:16:16.330538850Z", :model "glm-5.2"}